        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Clarify CPU-bound limitations of promises in long-running functions]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/42719</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/42719"/>
        <updated>2026-01-12T08:07:43.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/169522277?v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><p dir="auto"><span class="issue-keyword tooltipped tooltipped-se" aria-label="This pull request closes issue #42715.">Fixes</span> <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="3797857236" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/42715" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/content/issues/42715/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/42715">#42715</a></p>
<p dir="auto">This PR clarifies that wrapping CPU-bound work (such as generating prime<br>
numbers) in a Promise or using async/await does not make it run in parallel<br>
or prevent main-thread blocking.</p>
<p dir="auto">It explains the distinction between CPU-bound and I/O-bound tasks and points<br>
readers to Web Workers as the appropriate solution for heavy computation,<br>
helping avoid a common misconception for beginners.</p>]]></content>
